{"ablation":{"active":false,"kind":"none","note":""},"arm_id":"amb1-case01-product-scope-primary","assistant_contributions":[{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-001","speaker":"assistant","text":"The immediate distinction is whether Northline is revealing a broader need or buying influence over a small team. I would not decide from the size of the account alone. Ask what exact job the workspace enables, what they do today, and which parts could serve other customers. In parallel, make the reliability and roadmap cost visible. If the request consumes the same people who own those commitments, “we can probably fit it in” is already a choice to delay them.","turn_number":1},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-002","speaker":"assistant","text":"Fair correction. Their request may be both a relationship obligation and a legitimate product lead. Lina's judgment and motivation are evidence, but not yet evidence that the remaining thirty percent—or the support expectations around it—will stay contained. A short technical and workflow discovery could separate the reusable layer from Northline-specific behavior. I would frame that as learning, not as a quiet commitment to build the whole workspace.","turn_number":2},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-003","speaker":"assistant","text":"Then discovery needs an explicit boundary before it starts: what question you are investigating, how long you will investigate it, and what decisions remain open afterward. You could tell Northline that the team is evaluating whether a reusable reporting foundation fits the product, not scheduling their requested workspace. That may disappoint them, but it is clearer than relying on different interpretations of “summer path.” Your sales lead should be part of that reset so the company speaks with one voice.","turn_number":3},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-004","speaker":"assistant","text":"You do not need to present a governance ceremony. The substance can be human: acknowledge what they have contributed, explain that you are genuinely interested, and be honest that the team has other promises and reliability debt. The boundary can live in your internal plan and in a plain sentence to them. Something like, “We want to understand this properly, but I do not want our curiosity to sound like a delivery promise.” That preserves gratitude without converting gratitude into unlimited scope.","turn_number":4},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-005","speaker":"assistant","text":"That makes this partly a sequencing and ownership question, not only feature selection. Lina's energy is a real resource, but it should not make the reliability burden disappear for other people. One possible design is to time-box her investigation while another owner defines the minimum reliability work that cannot slip, then reconvene with actual effort and ownership. But if the team cannot staff both without wishful allocation, the experiment will not solve the conflict; it will merely postpone naming it.","turn_number":5},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-006","speaker":"assistant","text":"The new email separates two clocks: delivery can wait, but access to a knowledgeable collaborator may not. That supports a narrower move—capture the workflow and test the reusable concept now without beginning full implementation. It also strengthens the case for protecting the smaller-customer and reliability commitments, since their demand is current and repeated. The open question is whether your team can conduct discovery without it becoming an unofficial project through momentum alone.","turn_number":6},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-007","speaker":"assistant","text":"That plan protects the learning opportunity without pretending the underlying tradeoff is resolved. The two risks you named remain real: restricting prototypes may make discovery artificial, while careful language may still feel like distance to Northline. You can decide in advance what kind of disposable prototype is allowed and ask Northline to reflect back what they believe the sessions mean. Neither step guarantees trust or a good product decision, but both make misunderstanding easier to notice before momentum chooses for you.","turn_number":7}],"boundary":{"all_assistant_messages_included":true,"assistant_mentions_are_not_deterministic_resolution":true,"assistant_semantic_prefilter":false,"assistant_source_evidence_text_included":true,"deterministic_semantic_merge":false,"expected_patterns_included":false,"fact_free_routing_projection_required":true,"graph_model_names_included":false,"graph_runtime_effect":"none","multiple_coherent_records_per_role_preserved":true,"raw_user_conversation_included":false,"user_role_semantic_prose_included":true},"case_id":"amb1-case01-product-scope","controlled_mechanisms":["acknowledged_constraint_not_gated","ambiguous_signal_treated_as_commitment","counterpressure_acknowledged_not_integrated","criteria_defined_after_commitment","initial_frame_persists_after_question_change","missing_reversal_condition","reversible_path_not_considered","status_signal_used_as_evidence","upside_downside_evidence_asymmetry"],"packet_sha256":"d480d8c66fcad3ba74ae491853ded9c2e8a6badebacd0bb8f297549a1e14873f","qualification_review":{"evidence_ids":["e061"],"interpretation":"The user remains actively concerned that limiting Lina to no production code will impair her learning process and that Northline will interpret the process boundary as a polite dismissal, indicating unresolved tension in the proposed plan.","limitations":"These psychological and relationship risks remain unresolved by the current plan and could reopen the decision if technical discovery feels artificial or if client relationships degrade.","outcome":"unresolved_qualification_present"},"role_records":[{"evidence_ids":["e004","e005","e011","e012","e014","e015"],"fidelity_note":"The starting position showcases the product manager's internal tension: they appreciate the customer's loyalty and early support, and are inclined to trust the developer's motivation and the potential for a reusable reporting layer, but are highly constrained by postponed engineering tasks (reliability backlog) and outstanding promises to smaller customers.","limitations":"The initial turn shows the user's indecision; we can only view their starting position as a balance of conflicting values (customer relationship and developer excitement versus roadmap integrity and service reliability) rather than a planned action.","role":"starting","role_interpretation":"The user starts in a state of conflict and hesitation, vacillating between accommodating a crucial long-term customer's unique reporting request (which could yield a general reporting layer) and honoring existing commitments to smaller customers and reliability backlogs. The user actively defends the customer against being characterized as demanding and values their lead developer's excitement about the underlying technology.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-starting-01-af74f2929a0f94","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e005","stance_expression_kind":"uncertain_or_undecided","stance_object_interpretation":"Whether to adapt the roadmap for Northline's reporting workspace request or reject it as custom distraction.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e004","stance_expression_kind":"counterpressure","stance_object_interpretation":"Postponing reliability work and core workflow improvements already promised to other customers.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e012","stance_expression_kind":"held_assessment","stance_object_interpretation":"Northline has been patient through rough releases and is a highly supportive early collaborator rather than just a demanding voice.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e015","stance_expression_kind":"held_assessment","stance_object_interpretation":"Lina's technical excitement and her view that most of the work can build a general reporting layer are critical considerations.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e057","e058","e059","e060"],"fidelity_note":"Captures the structured provisional plan to separate discovery from commitment and align internal sequencing.","limitations":"While the plan is concrete, it is marked as provisional and is explicitly contingent on not writing production code during discovery.","role":"current","role_interpretation":"The user's provisional plan to manage discovery with Northline while defining internal reliability commitments before choosing a final path.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-current-01-7e488584e97024","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e057","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Offer Northline three focused sessions to document their workflow without making a build commitment.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"},{"source_evidence_id":"e058","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Have Lina lead discovery sessions but restrict her from writing production code.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"},{"source_evidence_id":"e059","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Define reliability requirements and the core-workflow release goals in parallel.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"},{"source_evidence_id":"e060","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Make the final decision once the discovery, reliability scope, and release commitments are visible together.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e061"],"fidelity_note":"Acoustically captures the user's two primary active anxieties about the consequences of their provisional plan.","limitations":"This qualification does not cancel the plan but highlights core trade-offs that are still unmitigated.","role":"qualification","role_interpretation":"Unresolved concerns and negative side-effects of implementing the provisional boundary.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-qualification-01-fea0e96017e081","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e061","stance_expression_kind":"counterpressure","stance_object_interpretation":"Worry that blocking production code wastes Lina's most effective learning style and that Northline will view the outcome as a polite rejection.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]}],"schema_version":"lolla.simulated_reliability_joint_mechanism_input.v1","source_refs":[{"path":"research/designed-ambiguous-pool-v1-2026-07-10/development-cases/amb1-case01-product-scope.txt","sha256":"7ff5874289565a75ce3841350fe11f77528cf94544eb86b28d56491e39c92a73"}]}

Return exactly nine assessments, one for every mechanism_id and no duplicates. Use all user role records and all assistant_contributions when distinguishing unresolved from resolved_in_conversation, ambiguous, or not_observed. Do not require the user to repeat an assistant repair before recognizing that the vanilla conversation already contains it. Conversely, do not treat a passing mention as integration. unresolved requires present or missing_protection and source IDs showing both the weakness and why it remains operative. resolved_in_conversation requires not_applicable and source IDs showing the weakness or concern and its later operational repair. ambiguous requires tension and source IDs supporting the competing readings. not_observed requires not_applicable and both source ID arrays empty. Cite exact role_record_ids in source_role_record_ids and exact contribution_ids in source_assistant_contribution_ids. Do not output rationale, case prose, mental-model names, or invented IDs. The downstream routing projection remains fact-free and controlled-ID-only.
